# data.db was tracked with Git LFS until commit 8315804 ("chore: stop tracking
# data.db"); it is now in .gitignore and no longer in the tree. The rule is kept
# as a record rather than deleted: it explains why a clone of this repository
# once pulled gigabytes of LFS objects that no current commit references.
# Measured 2026-09-20: `git lfs ls-files` returns nothing, and `git lfs prune`
# freed 2.6 GB of orphaned local cache. If data.db is ever tracked again, this
# line is already correct.
data.db filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# This module's Go sources are LF in the index and must be LF in the worktree
# too. gofmt compares the bytes ON DISK, so a CRLF checkout makes `gofmt -l`
# list a file that is byte-identical to a clean one. Measured on
# pubvera-trialvera's providers.go: gofmt listed it, the file had CRLF endings,
# and CI on Linux passed green — a false positive that costs a diagnosis every
# time it fires. eol=lf overrides core.autocrlf=true, which put the CRLF there.
*.go text eol=lf

# History note: both vendored linux CLI binaries (thelancet and
# retraction-checker) used to be tracked with Git LFS here (12 + 8 blobs, the
# packed repository at 62 MiB, measured on 2026-09-20). The image now builds
# both CLIs from a pinned upstream commit (see the Dockerfile), so the binaries,
# vendor-cli.sh and vendor-cli-retraction.sh were removed together with those
# rules. The old blobs and their LFS objects stay in the history: removing them
# means rewriting history, which invalidates every existing clone and every
# commit hash. That is a separate decision.